  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through next Friday)\nIssued at 1242 PM CDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\nOn Sunday night, the upper low will lift northeastward over the Four \nCorners, then eject into the central plains as a potent open trough \non Monday. This evolution should result in another mainly linear \ncluster of showers and storms over NE NM which may make it into the \nSW TX Panhandle late Sunday evening before dissipating. Despite the \nprogress of the upper low, moist and generally unsettled southwest \nflow aloft is set to persist over our region through Monday. This \ncombined with another shortwave disturbance lifting overhead will \nlikely bring another round of scattered showers and a few storms to \nthe forecast area through Monday afternoon before a general drying \ntrend takes place on Monday night. Warmer and drier weather is \nexpected during the Tue-Wed timeframe as flow aloft weakens and \nbecomes more zonal, and temperatures will again climb slightly \nabove normal during the middle of the week. Thursday and beyond, \nmodel consensus supports a return to a somewhat more active \npattern as deep cyclonic flow aloft overspreads much of the \nwestern CONUS. Details are scarce at this lead time, but this \nsignals a return of rain chances for the end of the week into next\nweekend.",
